1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,160
The diagram said encrypted. The implementation said otherwise.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:08,736
And the architecture review happened six months earlier.

3
00:00:11,736 --> 00:00:14,666
What if every architecture got a threat model that

4
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:17,948
shows its evidence, asks the questions it cannot answer,

5
00:00:17,948 --> 00:00:20,175
and never labels a guess as a finding?

6
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:21,816
That is what you build here.

7
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:28,536
Gennoor Tech presents the Agentic Architecture Reviewer.

8
00:00:31,536 --> 00:00:33,636
From diagram to threat register.

9
00:00:33,636 --> 00:00:36,523
Six STRIDE categories applied to every flow,

10
00:00:36,523 --> 00:00:39,608
but only to components that really exist in the

11
00:00:39,608 --> 00:00:42,036
model. Every edge carries its source.

12
00:00:42,036 --> 00:00:46,170
Every threat carries its assumptions and a validation question.

13
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:49,779
Every finding separates what was observed from what was

14
00:00:49,779 --> 00:00:50,304
assumed.

15
00:00:53,304 --> 00:00:54,670
Day one is the model.

16
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:56,880
You start with constrained inputs,

17
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:01,172
Mermaid flowcharts, a simple JSON architecture schema and Markdown

18
00:01:01,172 --> 00:01:04,423
decision records, and you report what could not be

19
00:01:04,423 --> 00:01:07,935
parsed instead of pretending. You build a typed system

20
00:01:07,935 --> 00:01:10,211
graph with components, data stores,

21
00:01:10,211 --> 00:01:15,608
identities, external systems, protocols, data classifications and trust boundaries,

22
00:01:15,608 --> 00:01:19,834
and you extract security requirements as explicit statements with

23
00:01:19,834 --> 00:01:21,720
I Ds and verification status.

24
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:26,547
Day two is the threat model.

25
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:28,962
The agent proposes STRIDE candidates.

26
00:01:28,962 --> 00:01:31,767
Your code accepts only those that link to a

27
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:35,030
modeled component or flow, and you watch it reject

28
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:38,619
a plausible-sounding threat about a service that is not

29
00:01:38,619 --> 00:01:42,143
in the design. Review rules then check authentication,

30
00:01:42,143 --> 00:01:45,536
authorization boundaries, secrets handling, logging,

31
00:01:45,536 --> 00:01:48,864
encryption, data minimization and failure handling.

32
00:01:51,864 --> 00:01:55,335
Findings are evidence-graded. Observed design facts,

33
00:01:55,335 --> 00:02:00,208
assumptions, missing information and recommended questions stay separate,

34
00:02:00,208 --> 00:02:03,746
and an unverified concern is never called a confirmed

35
00:02:03,746 --> 00:02:06,816
vulnerability. On day three architects accept,

36
00:02:06,816 --> 00:02:09,420
reject, defer or ask for more evidence,

37
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:11,289
with rationale and an owner,

38
00:02:11,289 --> 00:02:14,760
and the tool tracks changes across design revisions.

39
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:22,422
Then the cross-check. You compare modeled requirements with Terraform,

40
00:02:22,422 --> 00:02:26,751
Kubernetes and repository evidence, and report coverage and gaps,

41
00:02:26,751 --> 00:02:28,882
never full proof of conformance.

42
00:02:28,882 --> 00:02:33,544
Finally you evaluate against secure and intentionally flawed reference

43
00:02:33,544 --> 00:02:36,941
architectures: threat coverage, unsupported claims,

44
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:39,072
evidence linkage and usefulness.

45
00:02:42,072 --> 00:02:44,379
Labs use two reference architectures,

46
00:02:44,379 --> 00:02:46,810
one secure and one deliberately flawed,

47
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:49,491
so you can measure what the reviewer finds,

48
00:02:49,491 --> 00:02:52,296
what it misses and what it should have asked.

49
00:02:55,296 --> 00:02:57,783
Here is the full table of contents,

50
00:02:57,783 --> 00:02:59,631
9 modules over three days.

51
00:02:59,631 --> 00:03:03,469
Day one: Architecture inputs and a constrained format;

52
00:03:03,469 --> 00:03:05,814
The system graph with provenance;

53
00:03:05,814 --> 00:03:08,444
and Requirements extraction. Day two:

54
00:03:08,444 --> 00:03:12,637
Method-driven threat generation; Architecture review rules;

55
00:03:12,637 --> 00:03:15,480
and Evidence-graded findings. Day three:

56
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:18,180
Architect review and decision records;

57
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:22,800
Design-to-implementation comparison; and Evaluation and capstone.

58
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,972
Every build in this course follows six engineering principles.

59
00:03:29,972 --> 00:03:31,788
Model output is a proposal,

60
00:03:31,788 --> 00:03:35,287
never a fact. Authorization lives outside the model.

61
00:03:35,287 --> 00:03:38,247
A human approves every consequential action.

62
00:03:38,247 --> 00:03:40,535
Decisions and evidence are logged.

63
00:03:40,535 --> 00:03:44,505
The stack stays provider-agnostic. And quality is measured,

64
00:03:44,505 --> 00:03:45,312
not assumed.

65
00:03:48,312 --> 00:03:51,462
Three days, hands-on. Six STRIDE categories.

66
00:03:51,462 --> 00:03:55,829
Two reference designs. One hundred percent of findings linked

67
00:03:55,829 --> 00:03:56,688
to evidence.

68
00:03:59,688 --> 00:04:02,417
This course is for security architects,

69
00:04:02,417 --> 00:04:08,366
solution architects, application security, platform architects and engineering leads.

70
00:04:08,366 --> 00:04:10,885
You need system design fundamentals,

71
00:04:10,885 --> 00:04:15,084
basic networking and identity, threat modeling and Python or

72
00:04:15,084 --> 00:04:16,344
A P I familiarity.

73
00:04:19,344 --> 00:04:22,079
Review the architecture, not the slide.

74
00:04:22,079 --> 00:04:26,216
The Agentic Architecture Reviewer. Enrol now at gennoor dot

75
00:04:26,216 --> 00:04:26,496
com.
